# /etc/udev/rules.d/90-1bit-npu-power.rules - keep the XDNA2 NPU out of runtime autosuspend.
#
# Cycle 30, owner decision 2026-11-05 23:4x: this is the c30 fix for the take's `code 614` (the first dispatch
# into a context that was suspended and resumed hangs, `state 8, run 0 of 28`, then an `aie2_set_cmd_timeout`
# TDR). The 5 s autosuspend timer is what puts the array into that state, so the stick simply never suspends it:
# with `power/control=on` the resume path is never entered and the defect cannot be reached, without waiting for
# the driver-side fix (pi-kernel-74 owns that, off this cycle's critical path).
#
# The rule is the one this dev box has run since 2026-10-12 (owner, same reason: keep a persistent context, and it is
# why Ubuntu on that box never showed the 614), so it is copied measured rather than invented. The box's own comment
# line is kept above the rule, verbatim:
#   1bit (owner 2026-10-12): keep the XDNA2 NPU (AMD 1022:17f0) out of runtime autosuspend so the lane can keep a persistent context. The `TEST` guard means the rule is inert on a kernel whose device has no
# power/control attribute, and the vendor/device ids mean it never touches another device: PCI 1022:17f0 is the
# Strix/Krackan/Strix Halo NPU, class 1180 ("Signal processing controller"), measured here at 0000:c6:00.1 with
# driver amdxdna.
#
# `ACTION=="add|bind"`: on a normal boot the driver binds after udev is up, so `bind` fires; if the module were
# loaded before udev (initrd, or a coldplug that emits only `add`), `add` fires instead. /etc/init.d/04onboard-npu-keepawake
# is the fallback for the case where neither does, and it says so on the console when it has to act.
ACTION=="add|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="pci", ATTR{vendor}=="0x1022", ATTR{device}=="0x17f0", TEST=="power/control", ATTR{power/control}="on"
